WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:02.100
Filmmaking is a tricky business.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.910
It's a long journey from the script to the
screen, and a lot of great and not-so-great

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:11.340
scenes get left on the cutting room floor,
costing the studio time and money.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.440
Here's a look at some of our favorite films
whose deleted scenes came at a high price

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.370
— creatively and financially.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:21.070
World War Z's darker final act

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:25.390
World War Z didn't just trim an expensive
scene or two — this movie's entire third

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.510
act was gutted and replaced in post-production.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:33.350
In the theatrical ending, the movie concluded
with Brad Pitt's Gerry Lane wandering around

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:38.140
a research laboratory and discovering the
scientific solution to all the world's zombie

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.140
problems.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.070
But in the original shoot, things were supposed
to be a lot grimmer for our hero.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:48.739
Before the revisions, Gerry and his sidekick
Segen would've made it to Russia to do a blockbuster-sized

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:53.760
battle with zombies and discover a method
of undead-defeat the old-fashioned way.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.660
But there was still trouble on the homefront,
as his wife and children were in North America,

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.930
and she'd turned to prostitution to provide
for her family and ultimately shacked up with

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.790
his former colleague, played by Matthew Fox.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:10.140
This would've culminated in Pitt's character
questing across Siberia to make way to his

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.720
now-estranged wife, leaving room for what
would've been a full-on trilogy of World War

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:15.750
Z films.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.340
But the studio wanted a happier ending for
the film and brought in a bunch of new creatives

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.200
to rewrite and reshoot the third act.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.280
And everything else, including almost all
of Fox's parts, were left on the cutting room

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.670
floor, never to see the light of day.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.730
Not only did Paramount lose the money they'd
already spent for the clipped scenes, but

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:37.970
the re-shoots ended up costing a whopping
$25 million as well.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:41.390
And that, friends, is what you call a pricey
pivot.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.090
Rogue's scenes in X-Men: Days of Future Past

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:49.720
With its crazy time-travel plot, X-Men: Days
of Future Past is packed with familiar faces,

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:54.500
but unfortunately, that means limited screen
time for some of our favorite characters,

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.020
including Anna Paquin's Rogue.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:01.750
The True Blood actress spent a week filming
several elaborate scenes for the 2014 blockbuster

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:06.430
alongside fellow X-Men vets, but none of it
wound up in the finished film.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:10.869
Director Bryan Singer told Entertainment Weekly,
"Through the editing process, the sequence

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:15.209
became extraneous…like many things in the
editing process, it was an embarrassment of

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:18.159
riches and it was just one of the things that
had to go."

00:02:18.159 --> 00:02:24.290
After fan outcry, Singer and 20th Century
Fox released a Rogue Cut of the film on DVD,

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.780
restoring 17 minutes of previously cut footage
that included Paquin's sequences.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.739
The original tragic ending of Little Shop
of Horrors

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:38.099
If all had gone as director Frank Oz and screenwriter
Howard Ashman had initially planned, this

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:42.870
1986 adaptation of the Broadway musical wouldn't
have ended quite as happily.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:47.240
The pair intended to use the ending from the
stage version of Little Shop of Horrors, with

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:53.260
Audrey II eating Audrey and Seymour and terrorizing
the world — but test audiences hated it.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.260
"Feed me."

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.480
"Does it have to be human?"

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.459
"Does it have to be mine?!"

00:02:58.459 --> 00:03:02.731
Oz recalled to Entertainment Weekly, "For
every musical number there was applause, they

00:03:02.731 --> 00:03:06.609
loved it, it was just fantastic…until we
killed our two leads.

00:03:06.609 --> 00:03:09.919
And then the theater became a refrigerator,
an icebox."

00:03:09.919 --> 00:03:14.359
In order to get the movie released, they needed
better audience approval, so the elaborate

00:03:14.359 --> 00:03:19.900
23-minute sequence, which cost a reported
$5 million, was scrapped and a new ending

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.370
was shot with the two leads alive and well.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:28.150
Though a black-and-white version of the original
ending was included on a 1998 DVD release,

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:33.579
full-color footage was finally included in
a Director's Cut edition Blu-ray in 2012.

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:36.249
The Jitterbug dance number from The Wizard
of Oz

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:40.830
The Wicked Witch uses a lot of different tactics
to try to stop Dorothy and her friends in

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.829
The Wizard of Oz, but one never made it into
the final version of the film.

00:03:44.829 --> 00:03:50.019
In the lost "Jitterbug" sequence, Dorothy,
the Scarecrow, Tin Man, and Cowardly Lion

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:54.389
travel through the Haunted Woods only to be
attacked by evil bugs that cause them to break

00:03:54.389 --> 00:03:56.199
out in song and dance.

00:03:56.199 --> 00:04:00.659
Also a popular dance style at the time, the
"Jitterbug" was actually a remnant of an older

00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:03.029
version of the script that was tossed out.

00:04:03.029 --> 00:04:08.870
The whole number cost about $80,000 and took
five weeks to shoot, but it was cut when producers

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.669
decided the film was too long.

00:04:10.669 --> 00:04:15.879
Though the actual footage was destroyed, composer
Harold Arlen captured a dress rehearsal of

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:20.409
the number on video, later included as an
extra on the DVD version of the film.

00:04:20.409 --> 00:04:21.409
Who knows?

00:04:21.409 --> 00:04:25.259
Maybe the original footage will turn up in
a studio storage space like Dorothy's old

00:04:25.259 --> 00:04:26.960
ruby slippers did.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.360
Returning to Krypton in Superman Returns

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:35.240
Before DC and Warner Brothers decided to reboot
the Superman franchise yet again in 2013 with

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:40.569
Zack Snyder at the helm, Bryan Singer's 2006
entry starring Brandon Routh as the Man of

00:04:40.569 --> 00:04:44.370
Steel flew into movie theaters with...fairly
mixed reviews.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.810
Superman Returns also nearly arrived with
a completely different opening sequence.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.979
While the basic plot of the film deals with
Superman's return to Earth after a five-year

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:58.120
absence, Singer shot a wordless, visually
stunning five-minute opening sequence with

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.509
Routh's Superman visiting what was left of
his home planet, Krypton.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:07.210
The scene, which cost an estimated $10 million,
is slow and contemplative rather than bombastic

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.270
and exciting.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:11.430
Maybe that's why producers ultimately had
Singer cut it.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:16.759
The scene was finally released as an extra
on the 2011 Superman Anthology Blu-ray set,

00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:20.789
further dividing critics and fans on the one-off
superhero flick.

00:05:20.789 --> 00:05:23.729
Eric Stoltz's scenes from Back to the Future

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:28.910
As most Back to the Future fans know, Eric
Stoltz was originally cast as Marty McFly,

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:34.669
the puffy vest-wearing teenager sent back
to 1955 in a souped-up DeLorean.

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:39.379
Director Robert Zemeckis initially wanted
to hire Michael J. Fox, but his Family Ties

00:05:39.379 --> 00:05:44.159
shooting schedule was too demanding at the
time, and he hired Stoltz instead.

00:05:44.159 --> 00:05:48.910
Five weeks into filming, Zemeckis and producer
Steven Spielberg knew they needed to replace

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.629
Stoltz because he just wasn't funny enough.

00:05:51.629 --> 00:05:57.159
Zemeckis and Spielberg quickly negotiated
with Universal to replace Stoltz with Fox,

00:05:57.159 --> 00:06:02.940
adding a reported $3 million to the film's
budget and extra time onto its shooting schedule.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:07.819
Soon Fox was on set at the Twin Pines Mall
shooting with Christopher Lloyd, and the rest,

00:06:07.819 --> 00:06:09.220
of course, is history …

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:10.220
"Great Scott!"

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:12.030
"I know, this is heavy."

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:13.240
Thanks for watching!

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:16.340
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:18.550
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

